painter Cavaradossi as he awaits execution at dawn.\n10. \"Che gelida manina\" from Puccini's *La bohème* ends on a high C that many tenors transpose down a semitone.\n11. \"Sì, mi chiamano Mimì\" is Mimì's reply aria in *La bohème*, introducing herself as an embroiderer of flowers.\n12. \"O mio babbino caro\" from Puccini's *Gianni Schicchi* is the only aria in that one-act comic opera and is sung by the teenage Lauretta.\n13. \"Ave Maria\" from Verdi's *Otello* is Desdemona's prayer immediately before she is murdered.\n14. \"Va, pensiero\" from Verdi's *Nabucco* is technically a chorus, not an aria, yet became an unofficial anthem of Italian unification.\n15. \"Ridi, Pagliaccio\" concludes \"Vesti la giubba\" from Leoncavallo's *Pagliacci*, in which a clown must perform despite his heartbreak.\n16. Caruso's 1907 recording of \"Vesti la giubba\" is often cited as the first record to sell a million copies.\n17. \"Largo al factotum\" is written mostly in 6/8 and is a notorious test of an Italian baritone's diction.\n18. \"Una voce poco fa\" from *Il barbiere di Siviglia* was written for mezzo-soprano but is frequently sung by coloratura sopranos.\n19. \"Non più andrai\" from Mozart's *Le nozze di Figaro* mocks Cherubino's coming military life and was quoted by Mozart in *Don Giovanni*.\n20. \"Voi che sapete\" from *Le nozze di Figaro* is a canzonetta that Cherubino performs as a song within the opera.\n21. \"Dove sono i bei momenti\" gives the Countess in *Le nozze di Figaro* a nostalgic lament followed by a resolute cabaletta.\n22. \"Porgi, amor\" opens Act II of *Le nozze di Figaro* and is one of opera's most exposed entrances for a soprano.\n23. \"Madamina, il catalogo è questo\" from Mozart's *Don Giovanni* enumerates the seducer's 2,065 conquests, including 1,003 in Spain.\n24. \"Là ci darem la mano\" from *Don Giovanni* is a seduction duet whose theme Chopin used for a set of variations.\n25. \"Il mio tesoro\" from *Don Giovanni* contains a famously long melisma requiring extraordinary breath control.\n26. \"Ah! fors'è lui\" and \"Sempre libera\" form Violetta's double aria closing Act I of Verdi's *La traviata*.\n27. \"Libiamo ne' lieti calici,\" the *Brindisi* from *La traviata*, is a drinking song in waltz time.\n28. \"Di quella pira\" from Verdi's *Il trovatore* traditionally ends on an interpolated high C that Verdi never wrote.\n29. The \"Anvil Chorus\" of *Il trovatore* has gypsies striking anvils in rhythm at dawn.\n30. \"Celeste Aida\" opens Verdi's *Aida* and is dreaded by tenors for its soft high B-flat ending.\n31. \"Ritorna vincitor!\" shows Aida torn between her lover and her Ethiopian homeland.\n32. \"Cortigiani, vil razza dannata\" is Rigoletto's furious denunciation of the courtiers who abducted his daughter.\n33. \"Caro nome\" from *Rigoletto* is Gilda's delicate meditation on the name of her disguised suitor.\n34. \"Ombra mai fù\" from Handel's *Serse* is popularly known as \"Handel's Largo\" though it is marked larghetto.\n35. \"Lascia ch'io pianga\" from Handel's *Rinaldo* was recycled by the composer from his earlier opera *Almira*.\n36. \"Ah! non credea mirarti\" from Bellini's *La sonnambula* is sung by Amina while sleepwalking across a millwheel bridge.\n37. \"Una furtiva lagrima\" from Donizetti's *L'elisir d'amore* is a romanza in 6/8 accompanied by a plaintive bassoon solo.\n38. \"Ah! mes amis\" from Donizetti's *La fille du régiment* contains nine high Cs, earning tenors the nickname \"King of the High Cs.\"\n39. The Mad Scene \"Il dolce suono\" from Donizetti's *Lucia di Lammermoor* was originally accompanied by glass harmonica.\n40. \"Mon coeur s'ouvre à ta voix\" from Saint-Saëns's *Samson et Dalila* is Dalila's seductive plea before the betrayal.\n41. \"Je crois entendre encore\" from Bizet's *Les pêcheurs de perles* is sung largely in a floating head voice.\n42. \"Au fond du temple saint,\" also from *Les pêcheurs de perles*, is one of opera's most beloved tenor-baritone duets.\n43. The Flower Song \"La fleur que tu m'avais jetée\" from *Carmen* ends on a pianissimo high B-flat.\n44. \"Ride of the Valkyries\" opens Act III of Wagner's *Die Walküre* and is orchestral rather than vocal.\n45. \"Liebestod\" closes Wagner's *Tristan und Isolde*, resolving the harmonic tension opened by the famous Tristan chord.\n46. \"O don fatale\" from Verdi's *Don Carlo* is Eboli's cursing of her own beauty.\n47. \"Song to the Moon\" from Dvořák's *Rusalka* is the water nymph's plea to the moon to tell her prince of her love.\n48. \"Summertime\" from Gershwin's *Porgy and Bess* is a lullaby that has since become one of the most covered songs in history.\n49. \"Der Vogelfänger bin ich ja\" introduces Papageno in *Die Zauberflöte* with panpipe interjections.\n50. \"Nessun dorma\" gained mass popularity after Pavarotti's recording was used as the BBC theme for the 1990 FIFA World Cup.\n","path":"/var/tmp/ui/arias4.md"},"status":"ok","output":"Wrote 5989 bytes to /var/tmp/ui/arias4.md","summary":"/var/tmp/ui/arias4.md","started_at":1785914223144,"finished_at":1785914223144},{"type":"prose","role":"output","lts":[6],"src":[{"lt":6,"block":0}],"at":1785914225926,"markdown":"*Ecco fatto* — 50 arias inscribed in /var/tmp/ui/arias4.md.","id":"6.0"}],"from":0}],"more":{},"metrics":{"context_tokens":11569,"context_limit":1000000,"context_exact":true,"tokens_in":4,"tokens_out":2696,"cache_read_tokens":12499,"cache_write_tokens":7869,"mantra":"Use the write tool exactly once to write /var/tmp/ui/arias4.…"}}}}
